# Intent Chips Only Move Forward

A transcript is a path, not a menu. Once the member has moved past a row of
intent chips, that row is **history** and it leaves the thread: it fades,
collapses, and is removed. Only the newest row — the one that carries the
conversation forward — is on screen at all. This is app-wide and permanent.

## What this means on the surface

- Tap a chip → the turn plays, and the row you tapped fades out and is gone.
- Scroll back three turns → the answers are all still there, and so is every
  output they produced. The chip rows that led between them are not.
- The row trailing the newest answer is the only one there is.
- One step can own several rows. The signup multi-select posts its
  suggestions and its Continue / Skip controls as two rows, and the portfolio
  posts its report scopes as three — a run of **adjacent sibling** rows is one
  step, and the whole run stays until the member leaves the step.

## What is never removed

- **Output chips**, preview cards, version thumbnails, citation markers, and
  anything else that opens a module. An output the member never opened must
  still open three turns later — see the outputs-open-on-request rule. A row
  holding one of these **stays on the thread**, dimmed, with only its intents
  dead; it is the one case where the old disabled look is still what you see.
- **`.sc-inline-chips`**, the shared row the chat re-parks at the end of the
  thread after every reply. It is a single element that moves, so it is the
  live row by construction.
- Welcome chips and the persistent rail. They are not in the transcript.

## How it is wired

Shared module: `js/chip-lock.js`, injected from `js/agent-menu.js` on every
page that renders the WISE nav, and loaded directly by `create-account.html`
(no nav). It watches every transcript (`.chat-messages-area` / `#chat-messages`)
and stamps `data-chips-spent` on each spent row, then hands the row to
`retire()`, which stamps `data-chips-gone` and drops it once the fade and
collapse in `pages/wise.css` have run. A row matching `KEEP_SEL` is never
retired, and keeps the disabled look instead. Under
`prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the row goes at once, unanimated.

The height a row collapses from is measured in JS and written inline — CSS
cannot know it. A removal timer backs the transition up so a transition that
never reports finished cannot strand a dead row on the thread.

A row is spent when the member has said something beneath it (a `.sc-line-you`
landed below) or a newer chip row has arrived beneath it. This is one-way:
transcripts only grow, and a thread saved to history restores in the state it
was saved in.

## Rules for the agent

- Never bring a past chip row back, and never add a "replay this step" path.
- Do not fork this per page. A host that knows it has just left every open
  step calls `WiseChipLock.lockPrior(messagesEl)`; everything else is handled
  by the observer — see the nothing-page-local rule.
- A new chip-row class must be added to `ROW_SEL` in `js/chip-lock.js`, not
  handled with a page-local disabler.
- If a new control genuinely must stay tappable forever, add it to `KEEP_SEL`.
  That also keeps its row on the thread — do not opt the whole row out.
- A host must not hold a reference to a chip row across turns. Query for the
  last matching row when you need it; the earlier ones are gone.

## Verify

`scripts/_chip_lock_probe.py` drives two real turns and checks that every row
the member tapped through is off the thread, that the newest row is live, and
that the outputs the turns surfaced are all still openable.
`scripts/_chip_lock_shot.py` clips the thread in either theme.
